-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC # Migration Preparation
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Before beginning any UC migration project, let's cover some of the requirements that need to be satisfied before we begin.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC <div style="border-left: 4px solid #ff9800; background: #fff3e0; padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 16px 0;">
-- MAGIC   <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px;">
-- MAGIC     <span style="font-size: 24px;">⚠️</span>
-- MAGIC     <div>
-- MAGIC       This notebook is not supported for execution on Databricks Academy provided workspaces. The material is intended to be a study guide, to be run in your own sandbox environments if you would like. Databricks Academy does not provide an account with a live HMS, administrator level access, or an AWS account, all of which would be required to perform all the activities in these notebooks.<p>
-- MAGIC       <strong>The notebooks flagged as labs in the agenda can be run in a Databricks Academy provided Vocareum workspace and allow you to put the concepts into practice.</strong>
-- MAGIC     </div>
-- MAGIC   </div>
-- MAGIC </div>

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## A. Client-side Prerequisites
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC - **No hard blockers**: there aren’t many scenarios that would preclude a UC migration, but there are a couple you need to be aware of:
-- MAGIC    - Must be on the Premium plan or above (Unity Catalog is not supported in the Standard tier)
-- MAGIC    - If the customer is on AWS, they must be using E2 since Unity Catalog is not supported on single tenant Databricks (note that there are no such distinctions or limitations on Azure or GCP - Unity Catalog is automatically supported on those cloud platforms).
-- MAGIC - A vision of how their permissions will work in the new environment; having a clear vision will make initial setup a lot smoother.
-- MAGIC - **Identify administrators**: this is an important topic that we handle separately next.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### A.1 Identify Administrators
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Identify the various administrators on the customer side. You’ll be working with all of them. These include:
-- MAGIC - **Account admin**: manages workspaces, users, and groups, as well as many other operations performed during the upgrade
-- MAGIC - **Identity provider (IdP) admin**: integrates identity providers for automatic provisioning of users and groups
-- MAGIC - **Cloud admin**: upgrading will require access to the underlying cloud platform to configure resources. Specifically:
-- MAGIC    - AWS customers must be able to create S3 buckets, IAM roles, IAM policies, and cross-account trust relationships. 
-- MAGIC    - Azure customers must be able to create ADLS Gen 2 storage accounts, IAM roles, IAM policies, and service principals. 
-- MAGIC    - GCP customers must be able to create storage buckets and service accounts
-- MAGIC - **Workspace admin**: heavily involved in the upgrade, for the reconfiguration of users, groups, and the workloads processing data
-- MAGIC - **Metastore admin**: since there may not be one appointed already, you may need to draw attention to this role and its responsibilities. Access is also needed to the existing metastore, whether it’s:
-- MAGIC    - the Hive metastore included by default with each Databricks workspace, or
-- MAGIC    - an external metastore that they’ve integrated with their workspace.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Large organisations may have an individual or even a team dedicated to each of these roles, while smaller organisations may have individuals covering each (or many) of these roles. So be sure to get a clear understanding of who is covering what.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## B. Implementer Prerequisites
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Now as for you, the implementer, the one coordinating the upgrade representing the Databricks side, here are some things you should have going into this:
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC - Knowledge obtained through the **Databricks Data Engineering Professional Certification**, or equivalent experience.
-- MAGIC - **Python experience** is vital since many of the tools and code you’ll encounter will be written in Python. Having familiarity with that programming language will empower you to do a lot more, particularly since the upgrade process may call for some scripting and automation.
-- MAGIC - Many customers rely on Terraform for automation, particularly since it’s fully supported by Databricks. Therefore, knowledge of how to use Terraform is usually pretty handy.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## C. UCX Overview
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC **Unity Catalog Accelerator (UCX)** is a suite of utilities designed to simplify and automate the process of upgrading to [Unity Catalog](https://docs.databricks.com/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/index.html). Running it should be one of the first steps of any successful UC migration project.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### C1. UCX Prerequisites
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC UCX itself has technical requirements that must be satisfied before running:
-- MAGIC - A local compute environment with Python 3.10 or later and a recent version of Databricks CLI. This is the environment from where you will bootstrap the UCX installation process.
-- MAGIC - Network access from the compute environment to your target workspace, `pypi.org` and `github.com`.
-- MAGIC - Target workspace must be Databricks Premium or Enterprise with Unity Catalog enabled.
-- MAGIC - Credentials (a token) to access the target workspace with workspace admin privileges.
-- MAGIC - Identities must be set up at account level.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC ### C2. UCX Architecture
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC UCX is implemented as an extension to the Databricks CLI, and runs in an independent compute environment (like a VM instance, or your laptop). Within a Python virtual environment, it sets up an runtime environment that avoids conflict with the underlying system configuration. Using authentication furnished by the Databricks CLI, the UCX installation process connects with the target workspace and creates resources to implement the migration process, including dashboards, jobs, and possibly a supporting SQL warehouse. It also stores configuration info in files and sets up tables to store information that will be gathered during the assessment phase.
